WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:10.519
In a written statement signed by 181 of the
nation’s top CEO’s, the Business Roundtable

00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:12.590
signaled the end of shareholder primacy.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:18.130
Customers, suppliers, employees, and communities
are now among the new stakeholders.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.910
This raises several questions: How will firms
respond?

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.200
How will investment and shareholder returns
be affected?

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.770
Who wins in a conflict between a stockholder
and a stakeholder?

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.040
So far, equities markets have shrugged.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.410
It’s hard to determine the effect on stockholders.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:37.180
But, we can make predictions about which members
we expect this to impact the most.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.960
On one hand are firms where stakeholder engagement
is already part of their corporate strategy.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.640
For them, don’t expect much change.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.540
On the other, are firms in competitive industries
where it’s profit maximization or nothing.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:53.820
If they do anything other than focus on profits,
they won’t be in business for very long.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.379
The most interesting cases are the firms in
the middle, who do not deal directly with

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:02.320
consumers, but for whom brand is important
– or firms trying to rehabilitate a negative image.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.460
For them, expect changes to strategic decision
making.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.080
What do you think about the Business Roundtable's statement?

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.800
Let us know in our poll.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.300
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.420
and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

